<node id="1214260" title="Re^2: can sub check context for lvalue vs rvalue context?" created="2018-05-09 04:20:27" updated="2018-05-09 04:20:27" _doctext_mode="strict"><type id="11">note</type>
<author id="830549">haukex</author>
<doctext field_name="doctext">&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;i&gt;What would your wantlvalue return if called this way: &lt;c&gt;@x = x() = 5;&lt;/c&gt;?&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I think operator associativity can help answer that one:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
$ perl -MO=Deparse -e ' @x = x() = 5; '
@x = (x() = 5);
&lt;/c&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So I'd say that &lt;c&gt;x()&lt;/c&gt; is &lt;strike&gt;primarily&lt;/strike&gt; in lvalue context here. And what's being assigned to &lt;c&gt;@x&lt;/c&gt; is not the return value of &lt;c&gt;x()&lt;/c&gt;, but the return value of the [doc://perlop#Assignment-Operators|assignment operation], which is an lvalue itself.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;c&gt;
$ perl -le 'sub x : lvalue { $a } (x() = 4) = 2; print $a'
2
&lt;/c&gt;
</doctext>
